    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!-- R-1b: what keeps a long operation alive when the user leaves the app.
         Android 14+ requires the type to be declared here AND at start, and
         refuses the service outright if they disagree. POST_NOTIFICATIONS is
         requested because the notification IS the service on Android 13+. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!-- F5.3: explicit — the mic permission dialog can never depend on manifest merging. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />

    <!--
        F-6, the camera. Explicit for the same reason the mic is: a permission
        dialog must never depend on manifest merging.

        NOTHING is declared for reading photos. Choosing pictures goes through
        Android's Photo Picker, which hands over the chosen items and asks for
        no storage permission at all — so the app never holds the right to read
        the whole gallery, and there is no permission here to be audited,
        justified, or abused later.

        The camera is declared as NOT required so the app still installs on a
        device without one; the drawer entry simply returns nothing there.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />

    <!--
        Two permissions TALOS never asked for, removed.

        `ioninappbrowser-android` declares ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION and
        ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION, and manifest merging put them in the APK — they
        appeared in r13 and were absent in r12, found by dumping the permissions
        of both rather than by reading anything.

        TALOS does not use location anywhere. A distributed app that declares
        fine location it never reads is asking to be distrusted, and the owner's
        rule is explicit: GPS only with the user's say-so. If location is ever
        genuinely wanted, it gets asked for on purpose — not inherited from a
        library that happened to want it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" tools:node="remove" />
</manifest>
